While guesting on the Wednesday (Nov. 25) episode of Las Culturistas , the pop star opened up for the first time about the backlash she received following the visual&#8217;s release in August. The video found her in and out of auditions for a panel of overly critical men, changing things about her appearance and personality each time in futile attempts to please them.

By the end of the video, Grande realizes that none of those things should matter, and that the focus should have always been on her art. So when the public&#8217;s response to the &#8220;Petal&#8221; video ended up having nothing to do with the song or visual design, with people instead overwhelmingly commenting on the Grammy winner&#8217;s slender appearance, it felt like an episode of Black Mirror , Grande recalled.

&#8220;It was a fun thing that that video — with the intention of really spelling it out — had its own meta insane perception,&#8221; she told podcast hosts Bowen Yang and Matt Rogers. &#8220;It&#8217;s Black Mirror . I really thought it was going to be, like, a reveal, me sharing a vulnerable [part of myself].&#8221;

Shortly after the &#8220;Petal&#8221; video dropped, Grande&#8217;s rep announced that she&#8217;d be taking a break from the spotlight due to &#8220;endless, ongoing public scrutiny.&#8221;

Even so, she stands by the visual. &#8220;I&#8217;d put it out again today. I wouldn&#8217;t change a thing,&#8221; she said, noting how healing it was to bond with the dancers who played her fellow auditioners in the video over the song&#8217;s message. &#8220;We&#8217;ve all been told some kind of interesting thing about ourselves that has nothing to do with the art or our talent or our work. It was a very beautiful day on set.&#8221;

&#8220;It has always in the past hurt me a lot,&#8221; she added of having her intentions &#8220;distorted&#8221; by the public, something she explored heavily on her Billboard 200 -topping album petal . &#8220;Now I really just feel like as long as I&#8217;m creating from an authentic place &#8230; let the chips fall where they may.&#8221;

The singer&#8217;s appearance on the pod comes the day after she announced her Eternal Sunshine Tour documentary and concert film, coming Dec. 11 and 12, respectively. Eternal Sunshine Diaries: The Making of the Tour will &#8220;follow Grande during the 30 days leading up to the opening of her sold-out tour as she and her team race to bring the ambitious production to life,&#8221; according to a synopsis, while The Eternal Sunshine Tour captures the full setlist, visuals and choreography of her nightly show on the trek.

&#8220;Last time we did a tour doc, I was not really comfortable letting cameras into the room that much,&#8221; Grande said of the films on the podcast. &#8220;The Sweetener Tour doc that we did is not a doc. It&#8217;s the show with a few little silly bloopers in between. I didn&#8217;t really want to let people in at that time.&#8221;

The episode also arrives as Grande&#8217;s latest film project, Focker-in-Law — in which she stars alongside Robert De Niro and Ben Stiller — hits theaters.

